==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{compound|en|head|room}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The [[vertical]] [[clearance]] above someone's [[head]], as in a [[tunnel]], [[doorway]] etc; room (space) for one's head.
#: {{hyper|en|room#Noun|space#Noun|volume#Noun}}
#: {{cot|en|breathing room|elbow room|footroom|legroom|yardroom}}
# The vertical measurement, top to bottom, for example for clearance under a bridge.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1960|month=April|author=G. F. Fiennes|title=Unpunctuality - the cause and the cure|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=244|text=With a rare and beautiful ease one can now ring up a boffin, as I did the other day, and say: "As a price for not opposing our Parliamentary Powers for a new marshalling yard, the Council at X demands that the bridge over X Lane shall have 16 ft. 6 in. '''headroom'''. This means steepening our gradient from 1 in 70 to 1 in 65 for half a mile on a 20-chain curve. What difference will this make to the loads of Type "2", "3" and "4" diesels please?". Back comes the answer.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1963|month=April|title=Chepstow Bridge is rebuilt|journal=Modern Railways|page=265, photo caption|text=The new bridge (6) gives '''headroom''' of 13ft at high tide, sufficient for present-day river traffic.}}
# {{lb|en|electronics}} The [[ability]] of a [[system]] to [[reproduce]] [[loud]] [[sound]]s free of [[distortion]]; [[dynamic headroom]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2000|author=Wolfgang Ahnert; Frank Steffen|title=Sound Reinforcement Engineering: Fundamentals and Practice|page=177
|passage={{...}} the difference between signal-to-noise ratio and overload reserve ('''headroom''') as well as noise (safety) margin (footroom).}}
# The [[distance]] between the actual [[performance]] of an [[algorithm]] and its maximum possible performance.
# {{synonym of|en|headspace|gloss=space between the top of the contents of a container and its seal}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Beans, Greens, & Sweet Georgia Peaches: The Southern Way of Cooking Fruits and Vegetables|passage=For whole pickles and fruit preserves, leave no less than ½ inch of '''headroom''' at the top of the jars {{...|and cover them with the pickling or preserving syrup by at least ¼ inch, leaving an overall headroom of ¼ inch in the top of the jar}}|year=2014|author=Damon Lee Fowler|page=11|isbn=9780762792122|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=foKtBAAAQBAJ&pg=PA11|publisher=Globe Pequot Press|location=Guilford|edition=2nd}}

